        <tr><td>GitHub issues #1 and #2</td><td>Created and later closed with distinct reasons.</td><td>Track different problems with separate counts and outcomes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Signed incident review</td><td>Added a new <code>.svx</code> page.</td><td>Explain the evidence, exceptions, limits, and later update.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Original incident page</td><td>Corrected the one-cause claim and recorded closure.</td><td>Keep the entry page consistent with the review.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Triage page</td><td>Replaced automatic subject-based merging.</td><td>A shared subject is insufficient evidence of a shared cause.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision 0002</td><td>Added a superseding decision; left 0001 unchanged.</td><td>The README forbids rewriting accepted decision history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>IssueLinks component</td><td>Added computed counts, tracking links, and recorded outcomes.</td><td>Connect historical evidence to our new issues.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Analysis, renderer, tests</td><td>Added scripts and configuration.</td><td>Make the calculations and HTML reproducible.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Updating surrounding triage guidance and adding the replacement decision were my choices for consistency. The core assignment required issues and a signed review page; it did not explicitly require every surrounding edit.</p>
</section>

<section id="timeline">
  <p class="eyebrow">09 / How the session unfolded</p>
  <h2>The work included a few avoidable detours.</h2>
  <ol class="event-list">
    <li><strong>We established access.</strong>You approved GitHub CLI installation and signed in. I located your downloaded export and read its data and source.</li>
    <li><strong>I counted before rendering.</strong>I ran the KB classifier and got 379 + 33. I initially published the source review without its HTML, so the required visual artifact was missing.</li>
    <li><strong>You supplied the complete deliverable wording.</strong>I added the real Svelte/mdsvex renderer, generated the pages, and checked the HTML programmatically.</li>
    <li><strong>The first submission used the wrong field.</strong>The course refused the format. I corrected the answer to its required <code>repo</code> field, and the tickets passed.</li>
    <li><strong>You refreshed the course page.</strong>The export was dated 4 October. You supplied the later engineering update: #4471 closed 5 October; parser fixed; hint won't fix because booking-v3 was retired.</li>
    <li><strong>I recorded the separate outcomes.</strong>I closed the parser issue as completed and the hint issue as not planned, updated the KB, and regenerated the HTML.</li>
    <li><strong>The signature was wrong.</strong>I had used an author label. The course required its own issued proof. I retrieved it, replaced the signature, and regenerated the desk reference.</li>
    <li><strong>The course accepted the final repo.</strong>We saved the debrief. This visual walkthrough came afterward at your request.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="grid-2">
    <div class="card"><span class="tag">Parser</span><h3>Closed as completed</h3><p>The course's engineering update reported a fix: <code>fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales</code>.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="tag changed">Hint</span><h3>Closed as not planned</h3><p>The course reported that booking-v3 was retired. The hint was never corrected, so calling it fixed would misrepresent the outcome.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p>I should have obtained the full brief and proof requirements earlier, rendered before describing the work as nearly finished, and explained the reused components more clearly. I also started the local course log late; it includes reconstructed entries rather than a complete raw record from the first call.</p>
  <p>The final review preserves historical recommendations for correcting the hint even though its opening records retirement. Labeling those paragraphs more clearly as historical analysis would make the chronology easier to follow.</p>
</section>

<section id="verification">
  <p class="eyebrow">10 / Verification and limits</p>
  <h2>Acceptance and verification cover different things.</h2>
  <div class="grid-2">
    <div class="card">
      <span class="tag">Checked in the original solve</span>
      <h3>Nine automated tests</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Deduplication and identical overlapping rows.</li>
        <li>The expected cause counts and tenant coverage.</li>
        <li>Different causes for different channels.</li>
        <li>Unknown tenants and browser-locale independence.</li>
        <li>The five preserved unusual observations.</li>
        <li>Metadata and signature structure.</li>
        <li>Resolvable KB imports.</li>
        <li>Rendered counts, chart, styles, stamp, and outcomes.</li>
        <li>Working internal snapshot links.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="tag changed">Not established</span>
      <h3>The limits remain visible</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>We did not run the booking product.</li>
        <li>We did not independently verify engineering's fix or retirement.</li>
        <li>We did not resolve every unusual report.</li>
        <li>I did not visually inspect the HTML in an automated browser.</li>
        <li>The saved original explorer is not hydrated.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The course accepted the deliverables. That does not establish the broader checks above.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>The later engineering update and its date came from the course page you shared. The counts came from the export and classifier. The report makes that distinction because a source snapshot cannot establish what happened afterward.</p>
  <p>This walkthrough adds its own checks for rendered sections, evidence, offline links, and the absence of external assets. It uses native HTML navigation and disclosure controls so those features need no client-side framework runtime.</p>
</section>

